<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ibn Salim al-Aswani</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 969</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ibn Salim al-Aswani was an Egyptian official and writer of the 10th century, sent by the Fatimid commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawhar-al-siqilli">Jawhar</a> as envoy to the king of Makuria around 969. His account of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> survives only in quotations by later authors such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-maqrizi">al-Maqrizi</a>, and describes a prosperous and populous Christian kingdom along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>. It is a principal source for medieval Nubia.</p></div>
